    keywords: ['sensor', 'sensori', 'fan', 'ventole', 'watt', 'power draw', 'cpu temp', 'temperatura', 'librehardwaremonitor', 'pawnio', 'ffmpeg', 'admin', 'amministratore', 'elevated', 'permission', 'permessi', 'zero', 'empty', 'vuoto', 'fps', 'presentmon'],
    body: 'CPU temperature, fan RPM and power draw (watts) are read through LibreHardwareMonitor, and Windows only exposes those sensors to a process with administrator rights. Without that permission the values stay empty: it is not a bug. Since 5.1.0 Xenon itself never runs as administrator: the "Enable sensors" button (on the Fans/Energy widgets\' hint, or Settings → Performance → Hardware sensors) asks for one Windows confirmation and installs a small separate sensor service (files in Program Files, run at sign-in) that reads the sensors and hands the readings to Xenon. When LibreHardwareMonitor or its PawnIO driver is missing, the same place shows "Install sensors": the same confirmation installs both through winget (official packages). If winget is missing or the install fails, the card says so. After an update that changed the service, Settings shows "Update sensors", one confirmation. Installs that had the sensors on before 5.1.0 move to the service by themselves after the update, with no prompt. FPS in games needs its own Windows permission, granted by the same confirmation (see the FPS card). Regular CPU/GPU load and RAM never need admin rights. FFmpeg (voice chat, local voices, MP4 backgrounds, screenshots) is a separate one-click download on Windows in Settings → Performance, about 115 MB, no administrator rights.',
