    body: 'Xenon installs with one setup per system (Xenon-Setup-x64.exe on Windows, the DMG on macOS, AppImage, deb or rpm on Linux, the flake github:marcimastro98/Xenon on Nix and NixOS). The setup carries everything it needs: the first launch shows a short progress screen in the system language, with no terminal and no administrator prompt, and "Try again" if a step fails. Optional parts come later from the app, only when wanted: FFmpeg and the hardware sensors in Settings → Performance, PresentMon for in-game FPS. Older installs made with INSTALL.bat keep working and update as before. On Windows it registers a per-logon scheduled task named "Xenon Edge Widget" that starts the backend hidden at logon: deliberately NOT a Windows service, because a service cannot reach media info, hotkeys, audio or the microphone. The dashboard is served locally at http://127.0.0.1:3030. Updates: Settings shows when a new release is available; the self-updater downloads from GitHub Releases, verifies an Ed25519 signature before extracting anything (fail-closed), and if anything goes wrong it restores the previous version exactly and keeps the download so you can retry. User data lives in server/data and survives updates. After an update: a major version opens What\'s New full screen until it is closed; a patch or minor shows a card with what was fixed, once, and versions skipped arrive as one list; a failed update shows a card with the reason. With widgets only on the main screen the same cards appear in a corner. To see What\'s New again, ask Bit or click the version number at the bottom of Settings.',
